  "passages": [
    {
      "id": "passage-us-01",
      "document_version_id": "dv-nist-ai-100-1",
      "anchor_type": "pdf_text",
      "page_number": 7,
      "quote": "As directed by the National Artificial Intelligence Initiative Act of 2020 (P.L. 116-283), the goal of the AI RMF is to offer a resource to the organizations designing, developing, deploying, or using AI systems to help manage the many risks of AI and promote trustwor- thy and responsible development and use of AI systems. The Framework is intended to be voluntary, rights-preserving, non-sector-specific, and use-case agnostic, providing flexibil- ity to organizations of all sizes and in all sectors and throughout society to implement the approaches in the Framework.",
      "anchor_hash": "sha256:9d14cb88da2be575a8d36ccd2366c4d65208f21271f70e50ba77bb122e219a05",
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-us-03",
      "document_version_id": "dv-nist-ai-600-1",
      "anchor_type": "pdf_text",
      "page_number": 5,
      "quote": "A proﬁle is an implementation of the AI RMF functions, categories, and subcategories for a speciﬁc setting, application, or technology – in this case, Generative AI (GAI) – based on the requirements, risk tolerance, and resources of the Framework user. AI RMF proﬁles assist organizations in deciding how to best manage AI risks in a manner that is well-aligned with their goals, considers legal/regulatory requirements and best practices, and reﬂects risk management priorities. Consistent with other AI RMF proﬁles, this proﬁle oﬀers insights into how risk can be managed across various stages of the AI lifecycle and for GAI as a technology.",
      "anchor_hash": "sha256:615b647fbbc415b5df6123cecd118dc6801d3906b06978b32f9bb4622ad76ff7",
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-us-04",
      "document_version_id": "dv-caisi-overview",
      "anchor_type": "html_dom",
      "quote": "Establish voluntary agreements with private sector AI developers and evaluators, and lead unclassified evaluations of AI capabilities that may pose risks to national security. In conducting these evaluations, CAISI will focus on demonstrable risks, such as cybersecurity, biosecurity, and chemical weapons.",
      "anchor_hash": "sha256:219e4fd9b26170f216190e78b7fad9861b37e40d7b656decefcad8344169dc2c",
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-us-07",
      "document_version_id": "dv-omb-m-25-21",
      "anchor_type": "pdf_text",
      "page_number": 4,
      "quote": "U.S.C. § 901(b), and other requirements do not apply to elements of the Intelligence Community, as defined in 50 U.S.C. § 3003. b. Covered AI. This memorandum provides requirements and recommendations that apply to new and existing AI that is developed, used, or acquired by or on behalf of covered agencies.",
      "anchor_hash": "sha256:c045e47d8ed0cc96c49971dcadbf4161fbcb7930d1f1f6cb831e83fb9403a614",
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-us-08",
      "document_version_id": "dv-omb-m-25-21",
      "anchor_type": "pdf_text",
      "page_number": 15,
      "quote": "Conduct Pre-Deployment Testing. Agencies must develop pre-deployment testing and prepare risk mitigation plans that reflect expected real-world outcomes and identify expected benefits to the AI use. In conducting pre-deployment testing, if an agency does not have access to the underlying AI source code, models, or data, the agency must use",
      "anchor_hash": "sha256:e6ab6b37d63100ddbc57b5811d6d6a60efd99b657188887dc6354a5aaedcd2df",
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-us-09",
      "document_version_id": "dv-omb-m-25-22",
      "anchor_type": "pdf_text",
      "page_number": 9,
      "quote": "Testing and Evaluation. When evaluating proposals agencies must, to the greatest extent practicable, test proposed solutions to understand the capabilities and limitations of any offered AI system or service. As part of this work, agencies should consider whether it is appropriate to create a testing environment in agency networks specifically to enable testing ofproposed solutions on government-owned systems.",
      "anchor_hash": "sha256:9862e16c2b07b00201280573a8982525cca83cf2f9e01f0dcb9b0d2501d14d7a",
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-us-10",
      "document_version_id": "dv-omb-m-25-22",
      "anchor_type": "pdf_text",
      "page_number": 10,
      "quote": "E. Ongoing Testing and Monitoring. Contractual terms must provide the contracting agency the ability to regularly monitor and evaluate ( e.g., on a quarterly or biannual basis, based on the needs of the program) performance, risks, and effectiveness of an AI system or service. To achieve that outcome:",
      "anchor_hash": "sha256:42e794873e540128c5fa3440f2d6ff4bf456e0bec2ebb5a482cd4262126662f3",
      "language": "en"
    }
  ],
